<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosniaks</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bosniaks are a South Slavic people, mainly Muslim, native to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandzak">Sandžak</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and Montenegro. Their identity took shape under Ottoman rule, during which much of the population converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. Recognised in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> as a nation under the name Muslims from 1968, they adopted the name Bosniaks in 1993 and are the largest group in Bosnia and Herzegovina.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosniaks.jpg" alt="Bosniaks" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The authors of the final version of the officially adopted flag are Prof. Dr. Enver Imamović and graphic designer Zvo... · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bosnia_and_Herzegovina_(1992%E2%80%931998).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
